Task 1: Clone a VM from an Existing Template
Subtask 1.1: Verify Existing VMs and Templates
First, let's examine the current virtualization environment and identify available VMs that can serve as templates.

Connect to your Al Nafi cloud machine and open a terminal

Check the status of libvirt service:

sudo systemctl status libvirtd
Start libvirt service if not running:
sudo systemctl start libvirtd
sudo systemctl enable libvirtd
List all existing virtual machines:
sudo virsh list --all
Check available storage pools:
sudo virsh pool-list --all
Examine VM disk images:
sudo ls -la /var/lib/libvirt/images/
Subtask 1.2: Create a Base Template VM
Since we need a template to clone from, let's create a basic template VM first.

Download a minimal Linux ISO (CentOS Stream 9):
cd /tmp
wget https://mirror.stream.centos.org/9-stream/BaseOS/x86_64/iso/CentOS-Stream-9-latest-x86_64-boot.iso
Create a base VM that will serve as our template:
sudo virt-install \
  --name template-centos9 \
  --ram 2048 \
  --disk path=/var/lib/libvirt/images/template-centos9.qcow2,size=20,format=qcow2 \
  --vcpus 2 \
  --os-variant centos-stream9 \
  --network bridge=virbr0 \
  --graphics none \
  --console pty,target_type=serial \
  --location /tmp/CentOS-Stream-9-latest-x86_64-boot.iso \
  --extra-args 'console=ttyS0,115200n8 serial inst.text'
Complete the minimal installation following the text-based installer prompts:

Set root password
Create a user account
Configure basic networking
Install minimal packages
After installation, shutdown the template VM:

sudo virsh shutdown template-centos9
Subtask 1.3: Prepare Template for Cloning
Before cloning, we need to prepare the template by removing unique identifiers.

Start the template VM:
sudo virsh start template-centos9
Connect to the template VM:
sudo virsh console template-centos9
Inside the template VM, clean up unique identifiers:
# Remove SSH host keys
sudo rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*

# Clear machine ID
sudo truncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id

# Clear command history
history -c
sudo rm -f /root/.bash_history
rm -f ~/.bash_history

# Clear log files
sudo find /var/log -type f -exec truncate -s 0 {} \;

# Remove network configuration with MAC addresses
sudo rm -f /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-*
Shutdown the template VM:
sudo shutdown -h now
Exit the console (Press Ctrl+] to disconnect)
Subtask 1.4: Clone the VM Using virt-clone
Now we'll clone the template VM to create new instances.

Clone the template VM:
sudo virt-clone \
  --original template-centos9 \
  --name web-server-01 \
  --file /var/lib/libvirt/images/web-server-01.qcow2
Verify the clone was created:
sudo virsh list --all
Check the cloned disk image:
sudo ls -la /var/lib/libvirt/images/web-server-01.qcow2
Start the cloned VM:
sudo virsh start web-server-01
Verify the clone is running:
sudo virsh list
Subtask 1.5: Customize the Cloned VM
Connect to the cloned VM:
sudo virsh console web-server-01
Log in and regenerate unique identifiers:
# Regenerate SSH host keys
sudo ssh-keygen -A

# Generate new machine ID
sudo systemd-machine-id-setup

# Set a unique hostname
sudo hostnamectl set-hostname web-server-01
Configure network interface:
# Create network configuration
sudo tee /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp1s0 << EOF
TYPE=Ethernet
PROXY_METHOD=none
BROWSER_ONLY=no
BOOTPROTO=dhcp
DEFROUTE=yes
IPV4_FAILURE_FATAL=no
IPV6INIT=yes
IPV6_AUTOCONF=yes
IPV6_DEFROUTE=yes
IPV6_FAILURE_FATAL=no
NAME=enp1s0
DEVICE=enp1s0
ONBOOT=yes
EOF
Restart networking:
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
Verify network connectivity:
ip addr show
ping -c 3 8.8.8.8
