Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Clone Operation Fails
Problem: virt-clone command fails with permission errors.

Solution:

# Check libvirt service status
sudo systemctl status libvirtd

# Ensure proper permissions on image directory
sudo chown -R qemu:qemu /var/lib/libvirt/images/
sudo chmod 755 /var/lib/libvirt/images/

# Check available disk space
df -h /var/lib/libvirt/images/
Issue 2: Cloned VM Won't Start
Problem: Cloned VM fails to start with network or disk errors.

Solution:

# Check VM configuration
sudo virsh dumpxml vm-name

# Verify disk image exists and is accessible
sudo ls -la /var/lib/libvirt/images/vm-name.qcow2

# Check for conflicting MAC addresses
sudo virsh edit vm-name
# Remove or change the MAC address in the network interface section
Issue 3: Network Conflicts in Cloned VMs
Problem: Multiple cloned VMs have the same MAC address or IP conflicts.

Solution:

# Generate new MAC address for VM
sudo virsh edit vm-name

# In the network interface section, remove the mac address line:
# <mac address='52:54:00:xx:xx:xx'/>
# libvirt will generate a new one automatically

# Inside the VM, release and renew IP
sudo dhclient -r
sudo dhclient
Issue 4: Template VM Uses Too Much Disk Space
Problem: Template images are larger than necessary.

Solution:

# Compact the qcow2 image
sudo qemu-img convert -O qcow2 -c original.qcow2 compressed.qcow2

# Check image information
sudo qemu-img info compressed.qcow2

# Replace original with compressed version
sudo mv compressed.qcow2 original.qcow2
